# Line endings: LF in the repository AND in every checkout, whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, which checks text files out
# with CRLF. A shell script with CRLF does not run (`bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`), a
# systemd unit or a justfile may misread its lines, and a file compared byte for byte (the
# spec vectors) differs. `eol=lf` on a path overrides core.autocrlf for it. A repository
# generated from this starter carries this file too. docs/windows.md says more, and
# tests/test_windows.py clones the way Git for Windows does and checks.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly, so content detection can never decide them: the scripts, the shared
# vectors and the other data the tests read, the templates the generator copies, the
# service units and the rules.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.rq        text eol=lf
*.ttl       text eol=lf
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.plist     text eol=lf
*.recipes   text eol=lf
justfile    text eol=lf
